WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.340 align:start position:0%
 
mr. secretary thank you very much of the  time I've got a lot to ask you about  including your trip to Asia what's going  on in Ukraine but let me start first  with what happened at Fort Hood the  tragedy there you have any better idea  why ivan lopez opened fire on his fellow  American soldiers and why this is  happening so often well it is a terrible  tragedy and it's been revisited once  again on Fort Hood so our thoughts and  our prayers go out to the families of  the victims and those who have been  wounded and we're going to continue to  focus on them as we should but in the  meantime let the investigators do their  work we don't have a lot of the answers  we don't know why it happened we don't  know why he did what he did we will find  that out but this is 24 hours away from  that terrible incident so we're working  very hard assure first of all the safety  on all of our bases of our men and women  who worked there every day and and their  families so that they're reassured but  we'll find out what does it say sir that  America's military men and women are  finding themselves in harm's way back  here at home as well well it's a it's a  terrible statement I recognize that we  all do but like anything you we've got  to figure out what happened and what  went wrong we've been adjusting to and  implementing a number of recommendations  that came out of the 2009 Fort Hood  incident to mavey yard incident last  year to further bring more security and  further security to our bases and we'll  see what comes out of this as to how do  we assure in every way possible  the safety and security of those  those bases you've been meeting with ten  of your Southeast Asian counterparts are  going on to Japan China Mongolia you've  said and just told us here this is again  a sign of the commitment to the  rebalance to the Asia Pacific from the  Obama administration my question for you  sir is given what's happening in Ukraine  Syria American troops still in  Afghanistan is this really the right  time to rebalance towards Asia well I  don't think you can develop your  strategic interests based on one episode  or two episodes strategic interests are  driven by a nation's national interests  we've been a Pacific Power for many many  years a significant Pacific Power the  three largest economies in the world  border the Pacific China Japan the  United States we have treaty obligations  here we have some of our strongest  allies in this area we have tremendous  the important interests security  stability economic diplomatic interests  in this area so it's not a matter either  of choosing which part of the world  we're going to be in and which part of  the world we're not going to be in we're  not retreating for any part of the world  we have 400,000 men and women deployed  stationed all over the world in a  hundred countries were strong in NATO  we're going to continue to be the Middle  East but this is an area that is very  important to America and for our future  and for the Chinese and you're gonna be  mean your first trip is secretary to  China now you've been there before in  the past why shouldn't they take this  rebalance as a direct threat  well we've assured them in every way  President Obama has in his recent  meeting with President Xi mrs. Obama  being in China I have secretary Kerry  all of our administration's that this is  not a contained China approach this is  an approach to reach out to try to be  more open communique coordinate find  ways that we can work with each other in  a common purpose in common interests we  do that now with the Chinese and  areas will continue to do that do we  have differences yes we have differences  but the only way to deal with that is to  be direct open and I was invited to come  by the Minister of Defense I'm looking  forward to being there for three days  and talking about all these areas of  possible new cooperation and we'll talk  about where we differ as well let me ask  is you're on your way to Asia we still  have tensions in Europe the crisis in  Ukraine does not feel like we're  de-escalating at this point maybe the  opposite what's your sense right now  what evidence do you have if any that  the Russians those troops along the  Ukrainian border are backing down in any  way we're heading home we do not have  any verification that they are pulling  troops back there and then various  reports that they are moving troops back  there are troops that are moving around  we know that but no indication of any  significant movement of those large  troop deployments along that border away  from the border but the president's been  focused on a diplomatic resolution we  want to de-escalate this in every way we  can that's the smart way to do it the  responsible way to do it the Europeans  are coalescing now completely the EU  NATO around this effort and we made it  very clear what our position is here so  I think this is the responsible approach  and we'll continue to work at it it  feels like though that NATO may be  ratcheting things up you're sending  another US warship into the Black Sea  there's talk about NATO again  considering more options to sort of  reinforce the position in Eastern Europe  even the idea sort of maybe returning  one brigade and US Army Brigade back to  Europe that's been at least floated as  an idea could we do that would that be  something the Defense Department the the  US would consider sending an army we  gave back permanently to Europe well as  you know our European commanders supreme  Allied commander general Breedlove has  been tasked by NATO to come back to NATO  in about a week with a number of new  possibilities new measures new options  that we and NATO should consider and can  consider and and those areas that are  well are all part of the measures that  that could be considered but well let's  wait to see what general Breedlove comes  back with do you get a sense are you  worried this is getting gonna become  worse before it gets better  no I don't think there's any escalation  on our part we told the Russians there  would be consequences for their actions  we've taken economic sanctions the EU  the United United States and so we've  been very clear what measures we would  take the Obama administration misjudge  Vladimir Putin and the challenge posed  by Russia going forward I don't think  anybody misjudged  light of our Putin unless let's go back  to 2008 when President Putin invaded  Georgia so this isn't a particularly new  approach that he's taken but what you  always have to do is you have to always  be prepared for any contingencies any  possibilities but I don't think it was a  matter of a misjudging Putin what his  intentions were why he did what he did  we we still don't understand fully but  the point of responsible governments is  to have strong options and capabilities  and be prepared for any contingency it's  a dangerous world out there and it's a  very unpredictable world and you're  having to negotiate that at an  environment of dwindling defence dollars  of tough budgets you just presented one  as the situation in Ukraine the the  crisis there  led you to reassess even the budget you  just submitted would you do things  differently now knowing what's happening  well let's not forget the number that I  presented in the president's budget was  not my number was that the president's  number that was that was a number that  was already agreed to by Congress  in the bipartisan Budget agreement in  December  so the 496 billion dollar budget  you've already already we've already  presented a bigger number the budget  that I presented President Obama's  budget was a bigger number and we've  asked for a hundred and sixteen billion  dollars more than what the numbers are  right now based on sequestration in the  next two years and for this year a  twenty six billion dollar increase in  the budget as well so does President  Obama have made that very clear as I  have does this crisis in some strange  way make your case for you that these  numbers need to be higher could you the  Ukraine crisis the challenge posed by  Russia ultimately helped Chuck Hagel get  the kind of budget you want well I think  the way you look at it is not just one  episode but what is it going to take to  fulfill the missions and obligations of  our Defense Department budget in order  to assure the security of the United  States of America we have weeks of  continued testimony we've got senior  members today up on Capitol Hill  explaining to the Congress what we need  in the way of resources in order to  fulfill those missions to assure the  United States the people of the United  States that we can do the job that they  want us to do to assure of their  security so we believe it's going to  take increased resources we've laid that  out we've given the numbers and we've  given the rise now you're on your waiter  [ __ ] you're the first defense secretary  to go there since Don Rumsfeld about ten  years ago when he got there what he left  they gave him a gift of a horse my  question is are you prepared for a  similar gift and is there room on the  plane well I don't know if my guys would  consider give me a horse but I would be  privileged if if they give me any  recognition of our partnership the  United States and Mongolia but probably  there's no place for livestock that I've  seen on that plane

